         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### Roads\n\nLong before modern highways, the indigenous peoples of the Americas (*Abya Yala*) were master road builders. The Mayas created **sacbes**—white stone roads that connected their cities like threads in a web.\n\nEven more impressive, the Incas built the **Qhapaq Ñan**, a road network stretching 39,000 kilometers across mountains, deserts, and forests. These roads were the arteries of their empire, carrying people, goods, and ideas.\n\nThink about it: roads connect places, just like numbers connect to each other. A road takes you from one city to the next—can numbers do the same?",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
